cartridge "WORD HUNT/COMPUTE!S GAZETTE/1983"

' WORD HUNT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Word Hunt", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, July 1983
' (Commodore 64 and VIC-20, BASIC; disk 1983-07 files "word hunt.64" and
' "word hunt.vic", orig/). No author is named in the listing; the disk was
' retyped by a reader around 1988 and the article was not available.
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: ten words are hidden in a 10 x 10 grid of letters, across,
' down or diagonally, forward or backward. They are asked for one at a
' time, longest first. Move the box with the stick to the word's first
' letter and press the button, then to its last letter and press again.
' Quick answers score more: within the skill's time 100 points, within 20
' times that 10 points, later 5 or less. A wrong answer is NO: the word's
' place is shown in red and the next word comes. After ten words the game
' ends with the score.
'
' From the original (orig/word hunt.64.bas): a 10 x 10 grid with row and
' column numbers 0-9, ten words of 3-8 letters typed in, sorted longest
' first (ties in entry order), each placed at a random cell in one of 8
' directions (1 up-right, 2 right, 3 down-right, 4 down, 5 down-left,
' 6 left, 7 up-left, 8 up: the 7 8 1 / 6 Q 2 / 5 4 3 compass beside the
' grid), crossing others only on the same letter; the words are asked in
' that order, one each, with the clock (TI$) reset; the player types the
' ROW and COLUMN of the first letter and the direction; an answer is right
' when the letters from there spell the word (so a chance copy counts);
' points by the time WT in jiffies with SL = 9 - SKILL: WT < SL*60 gives
' 100, WT < SL*1200 gives 10, else 5+INT((SL*1200-WT)/60) (line 1380, whose
' parentheses don't balance in the retyped listing); a wrong answer shows
' the right ROW,COL and DIR with NO and waits for a key; SCORE total
' beside the grid; after the tenth word, play again? (Y/N). Skill changes
' only the timing (grid, words and directions are the same at every
' level).
'
' Liberties: no typing: six built-in themed lists (ANIMALS, FOOD, SPORTS,
' SPACE, OCEAN, COMPUTER; 24 words of 3-8 letters each), ten random words
' a game; the player marks the first and the last letter with a box
' instead of typing ROW, COL and DIR (the clock runs until the last press);
' a press on a cell that is not on one of the eight lines, or at the wrong
' length, only buzzes (the C64 asked again for a bad direction key); the
' marked line shows in yellow and the compass digit of its direction is
' boxed; found words stay green, missed words red; the panel shows the
' seconds and what the word is worth now; the late score never goes below
' 0 (the C64 line would have gone negative, if it ran); filler letters
' A-Z (the C64's INT(25*RND(1)+65) never gave Z); skill limited to 1-5
' (the C64 test IF X<1 AND X>5 let anything through); a grid that will not
' fit is made again (the C64 asked for new words); no play again question:
' a button returns to the title.
'
' Controls: either controller. Stick moves the box (diagonals too; it
' repeats when held), the left button marks the first and the last
' letter, the right button (or the left on the first letter again) lets
' go. Title: up/down choose; setup: up/down a line, left/right change it.
'
' House rules: text and tiles in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code
' 128; screens drawn with the NMI off; music first in the sound table,
' effects after, the title tune played once; `set sprite count` always
' given. Nothing flashes.

